To reproduce the issue initialize a new cargo project and put following code into the `main.rs`:

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("Hello Tarpaulin!");
}

fn get_entries_from_vector_using_string_ids<T>(vector: &[T], string_ids: &[String]) -> Vec<T>
where
    T: Clone,
{
    let mut searched_entries = Vec::<T>::new();

    for id in string_ids {
        let id = match id.parse::<usize>() {
            Ok(value) => value,
            Err(_) => {
                println!("'{}' is not a valid number.", id);
                continue;
            }
        };

        if id == 0 {
            println!("'0' is no valid id.");
            continue;
        }

        let id = id - 1;

        if id >= vector.len() {
            println!("There is no element with id '{}'.", id + 1);
            continue;
        }

        searched_entries.push(vector[id].clone())
    }

    searched_entries
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    #[test]
    fn test() {
        // Arrange
        let vector = vec![
            String::from("Spongebob"),
            String::from("Patrick"),
            String::from("Taddeus"),
        ];

        let ids = vec![
            String::from("0"),
            String::from("a"),
            String::from("2"),
            String::from("3"),
            String::from("4"),
        ];

        // Act
        let result = get_entries_from_vector_using_string_ids(&vector, &ids);

        // Assert
        assert_eq!(
            result,
            vec![String::from("Patrick"), String::from("Taddeus")]
        );

        // uncomment to force test to fail to show print statements
        assert!(false);
    }
}
```
Running `cargo tarpaulin` on the project results in following output:
```
running 1 test
test tests::test ... ok

test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s

2026-09-11T06:54:43.075938Z  INFO cargo_tarpaulin::report: Coverage Results:
|| Uncovered Lines:
|| src/main.rs: 2, 14, 16, 22, 29
|| Tested/Total Lines:
|| src/main.rs: 14/19 +0.00%
||
73.68% coverage, 14/19 lines covered, +0.00% change in coverage
```

But when forcing the test to fail by uncommenting line 68 running `cargo tarpaulin` again gives the following output:
```
running 1 test
test tests::test ... FAILED

failures:

---- tests::test stdout ----
'0' is no valid fuffle id.
'a' is not a valid number.
There is no fuffle with id '4'.

thread 'tests::test' (15966) panicked at src/main.rs:68:9:
assertion failed: false
note: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace


failures:
    tests::test

    test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s

    Error: "Test failed during run"
```

This clearly proofs that that the lines 16, 22 and 29 should be covered.

In addition the line 14 is reported as uncovered. This can be "resolved" by using the returned error value, for example by printing it.

I am running this code on following distribution:
```
$ uname -a
Linux <censored> 7.1.8-arch1-3 #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Tue, 11 Aug 2026 09:16:08 +0000 x86_64 GNU/Linux
```

These issues only occur when using a generic definition of the function `get_entries_from_vector_using_string_ids`. If you remove the `<T>` and replace its references by `String` the lines are printed as covered.

This bug can be reproduced on tarpaulin version 37.2.
